=== layout: subsection

# Reading Chromosome Addresses

=== layout: one-panel

# Read an Address

@body

[*Gene address*]{color=#24578F}: like a book's library address

{table-style=comparison text-align=left}
| Part | What It Names | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Chromosome | Which chromosome? | 7 |
| Arm | Short p or long q? | q |
| Region | Which region of that arm? | 3 |
| Band | Which band in that region? | 1 |
| Subband | Which part of that band? | .2 |

=== layout: one-panel

# Try It: The CFTR Address

@body

[*CFTR*: *7q31.2*]{color=#24578F}

- Which chromosome?
- Which arm?
- Which region?
- Which band?
- Which subband?

=== layout: two-panels

# CFTR: Read Each Part

@left

{table-style=comparison text-align=left}
| Address Part | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| [*7*]{color=#D40000} | Chromosome 7 |
| [*q*]{color=#B74300} | Long arm |
| [*3*]{color=#007A00} | Region 3 |
| [*1*]{color=#003FFF} | Band 1 |
| [*.2*]{color=#A719DB} | Subband 2 |

@right

![CFTR chromosome address hierarchy: 7q31.2](assets/visual_renewal/cftr_address.drawn.svg)

@citation

https://medlineplus.gov/genetics/understanding/howgeneswork/genelocation/

=== layout: one-panel

# Where Is CFTR?

@body

![Original chromosome 7 band-location illustration for CFTR at 7q31.2](assets/lect10c-chromo_nomenclature/image_012.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# Nearer or Farther?

@body

[*Higher band numbers*]{color=#24578F}: farther from the centromere

{table-style=comparison}
| Same Arm | Nearer the Centromere | Farther from the Centromere |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Chromosome 14, q arm | [*14q21*]{color=#24578F} | [*14q22*]{color=#B74300} |

- Compare chromosome and arm first
- Then compare region, band, and subband
- [*Band label*]{color=#24578F}: address, not distance in cM

@citation

https://medlineplus.gov/genetics/understanding/howgeneswork/genelocation/

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

On chromosome *17q*, which band is most distal (farthest from the centromere)?

- A. 17q36
- B. 17q36.3
- C. 17q11
- D. 17q34.1
- E. 17q41.3

[*Numbering practice: schematic labels*]{color=#24578F}

@answer

E. 17q41.3

=== layout: one-panel

# Band Order: Answer

@body

- Same chromosome and arm: [*17q*]{color=#24578F}
- Region first: [*4*]{color=#007A00} farther out than *3* or *1*
- Region 4: [*17q41.3*]{color=#007A00}
- [*Answer: E. 17q41.3*]{color=#007A00}

*Schematic labels; not a real chromosome 17 band map*

=== layout: subsection

# Reading a Karyotype

=== layout: one-panel

# Three Parts of a Simple Karyotype

@body

{table-style=comparison text-align=left}
| Part | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| [*47*]{color=#D40000} | Number of chromosomes |
| [*XY*]{color=#B74300} | Sex-chromosome complement |
| [*+21*]{color=#003FFF} | Extra copy of chromosome 21 |

- [*+21*]{color=#003FFF}: extra *whole chromosome 21*
- [*7q31.2*]{color=#24578F}: chromosome address

@citation

https://medlineplus.gov/genetics/condition/down-syndrome/

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

What does *47,XY,+21* describe?

- A. XX with two extra copies of chromosome 21
- B. XY with a partial deletion of chromosome 21
- C. XY with an extra pair of chromosome 21
- D. XX with one extra copy of chromosome 21
- E. XY with one extra copy of chromosome 21

@answer

E. XY with one extra copy of chromosome 21

=== layout: one-panel

# Karyotype: Answer

@body

- [*47*]{color=#D40000}: one more than 46
- [*XY*]{color=#B74300}: sex-chromosome complement
- [*+21*]{color=#003FFF}: one extra chromosome 21
- [*Answer: E*]{color=#007A00}
- Three copies: [*trisomy 21*]{color=#003FFF}

Homework notation: *47,+21,XY* -- same example, different order

